Each algorithm of Task 1 becomes one subprogram: a `void` function in ANSI C, a `procedure` in Pascal. The matrix is the first parameter and its size n the second. In C an array is passed by address, so the function changes the caller's matrix; in Pascal the matrix is a `var` parameter, for the same effect. Rows and columns are numbered from 1, exactly as in the algorithms (the C array has one extra row and one extra column that are never used).

**ANSI C**

```c
#include <stdio.h>

#define MAXN 15              /* largest size accepted for the matrix */

/* Rows and columns are numbered from 1, as in the algorithms (index 0 is not used). */
typedef long Matrix[MAXN + 1][MAXN + 1];

/* Algorithm 1(c): first column = 1, all the other columns = 0 */
void set_first_column_and_zeros(Matrix A, int n)
{
    int i, j;

    for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        A[i][1] = 1;
        for (j = 2; j <= n; j++) {
            A[i][j] = 0;
        }
    }
}

/* Algorithm 1(b): leading diagonal = 1 */
void set_diagonal(Matrix A, int n)
{
    int i;

    for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        A[i][i] = 1;
    }
}

/* Algorithm 1(d): inside of the triangle, zeros beyond the diagonal */
void fill_inside(Matrix A, int n)
{
    int i, j;

    for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        for (j = 2; j <= i - 1; j++) {
            A[i][j] = A[i - 1][j] + A[i - 1][j - 1];
        }
        for (j = i + 1; j <= n; j++) {
            A[i][j] = 0;
        }
    }
}
```

**Standard Pascal**

```pascal
program PascalTriangle(input, output);

const
  MAXN = 15;                       { largest size accepted for the matrix }

type
  Matrix = array[1..MAXN, 1..MAXN] of integer;

var
  Tri : Matrix;
  n   : integer;

{ Algorithm 1(c): first column = 1, all the other columns = 0 }
procedure SetFirstColumnAndZeros(var A : Matrix; n : integer);
var
  i, j : integer;
begin
  for i := 1 to n do
  begin
    A[i, 1] := 1;
    for j := 2 to n do
      A[i, j] := 0
  end
end;

{ Algorithm 1(b): leading diagonal = 1 }
procedure SetDiagonal(var A : Matrix; n : integer);
var
  i : integer;
begin
  for i := 1 to n do
    A[i, i] := 1
end;

{ Algorithm 1(d): inside of the triangle, zeros beyond the diagonal }
procedure FillInside(var A : Matrix; n : integer);
var
  i, j : integer;
begin
  for i := 1 to n do
  begin
    for j := 2 to i - 1 do
      A[i, j] := A[i - 1, j] + A[i - 1, j - 1];
    for j := i + 1 to n do
      A[i, j] := 0
  end
end;
```

Points to check in the translation:

- `For i ← 1 To n` becomes `for (i = 1; i <= n; i++)` in C and `for i := 1 to n do` in Pascal; the limits are the same, so the same cells are visited.
- `A[i, j]` becomes `A[i][j]` in C and stays `A[i, j]` in Pascal.
- When i = 1 or 2 the loop `j = 2 .. i - 1` runs zero times (in C the test `j <= i - 1` is false at once; in Pascal a `for` whose start is greater than its end is skipped), so `A[0][...]` is never read.
- In C, `typedef long Matrix[...]` makes `Matrix A` in a parameter list an array parameter (a pointer to a row); the values written by the function are kept after the call.

**Check.** The three subprograms were translated line for line into Python (same bounds, same order of the statements), run for n from 1 to 15 in the order (c), (b), (d), and every matrix obtained was compared with the binomial coefficients: all agree. The C and Pascal text was **not compiled** (no compiler available); it was written with the declarations, the semicolons and the `begin`/`end` pairs checked by hand.

**Marking (9 marks):** FillFirstColumnAndZeros (3 marks: the subprogram header with the matrix and n, the two loops, the two assignments); SetDiagonal (2 marks: header and loop, assignment); FillInside (4 marks: the two loops on the rows and on j, the sum formula with the right indices, the zeros beyond the diagonal).

To print a matrix, the rows are printed one after the other and, in each row, the elements one after the other on the same line; a new line is started at the end of every row. Two nested loops are used (rows outside, columns inside), and each number is printed in a field of width 6 so that the columns line up.

**ANSI C**

```c
/* Task 2(b): print all the elements of the n x n matrix, one row per line */
void print_matrix(Matrix A, int n)
{
    int i, j;

    for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        for (j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
            printf("%6ld", A[i][j]);
        }
        printf("\n");
    }
}
```

**Standard Pascal**

```pascal
{ Task 2(b): print all the elements of the n x n matrix, one row per line }
procedure PrintMatrix(var A : Matrix; n : integer);
var
  i, j : integer;
begin
  for i := 1 to n do
  begin
    for j := 1 to n do
      write(A[i, j] : 6);
    writeln
  end
end;
```

- `printf("%6ld", A[i][j])` prints a `long` right-aligned in 6 characters; the matrix holds `long` values, hence `%ld` and not `%d`. `printf("\n")` ends the line after the inner loop.
- In Pascal `write(A[i, j] : 6)` gives the same field width, and `writeln` with no argument ends the line.
- The subprogram does not change the matrix: it only reads the elements.

**Result for a 3 x 3 matrix** (the three filling procedures of (a) applied, then the printing loops; simulation in Python of the same loops and of the same width):

```
     1     0     0
     1     1     0
     1     2     1
```

**Marking (3 marks):** a subprogram with the matrix and its size as parameters (1 mark); two nested loops covering all the rows and all the columns (1 mark); a new line after each row, so that the matrix appears as a table (1 mark).

